+** 2.9. Timed reservation over a resource - two coupled machines + interval scheduling
+
+- *Instance*: experiment 004 (proposed challenge 10 timed-reservation). It uses
+  conference rooms, with the no-show release. Stretch variant: the DHCP lease
+  (RFC 2131 figure 5). Backward link: inventory allocation (see the note on
+  sec 2.1 below). Downstream instance: =aygp-dr/display-reservation=.
+- *Structure*: two state machines share a resource key, and a time axis sits on
+  top of them. Neither the picker nor a plain state chain has a time axis.
+  - The *resource lifecycle* R says whether the thing can be reserved at all.
+    Its initial state is =acquired= and its sole terminal is =retired=. By its
+    edge table, R is *cyclic*: =available, in-service, maintenance,
+    out-of-order= lie on cycles, for example =available -> maintenance ->
+    available= and =available -> in-service -> available=. R is a sec 2.2
+    cycle.
+  - The *reservation lifecycle* V says what one party holds against the
+    resource over an interval =[start, end)=. By its edge table, V is *acyclic*,
+    with terminals =completed, cancelled, expired, no-show=. V is a sec 2.4 DAG
+    with terminals. =overdue= is a live, non-terminal exit: it still holds the
+    resource, and it ends at =check_out=.
+  - =check_in= and =check_out= are *shared events*. They form a synchronous
+    product: one event moves both machines, so =in-service= is the
+    resource-side image of =active= / =overdue=.
+  - =reassign= is a *key-changing* event, not a V edge. It rebinds a held or
+    confirmed reservation to another resource instance, and its V state does
+    not change.
+
+Resource lifecycle R. These are the legal edges; any other (from, event) pair
+is an illegal transition (T2). =writer= is the source allowed to write the
+event. The write side enforces it (sec 3.1 E7), and the projector reports a
+violation as S1.
+
+| from | event | to | writer |
+|-+-+-+-|
+| acquired | commission | available | operator |
+| available | check_in | in-service | party |
+| in-service | check_out | available | party/operator |
+| available | maintain | maintenance | scheduler/operator |
+| out-of-order | maintain | maintenance | scheduler/operator |
+| maintenance | restore | available | scheduler/operator |
+| out-of-order | restore | available | scheduler/operator |
+| available | fault | out-of-order | operator |
+| in-service | fault | out-of-order | operator |
+| available | retire | retired | operator |
+| maintenance | retire | retired | operator |
+| out-of-order | retire | retired | operator |
+
+Reservation lifecycle V. These are the legal edges. =request= creates the
+reservation. The last row is the key-changing =reassign=, which is not an edge.
+
+| from | event | to | writer |
+|-+-+-+-|
+| (none) | request | requested | party |
+| requested | hold | held | party |
+| requested | cancel | cancelled | party/operator |
+| requested | expire | expired | scheduler |
+| held | confirm | confirmed | party |
+| held | cancel | cancelled | party/operator |
+| held | expire | expired | scheduler |
+| confirmed | check_in | active | party |
+| confirmed | cancel | cancelled | party/operator |
+| confirmed | no_show | no-show | scheduler |
+| active | check_out | completed | party/operator |
+| active | overdue | overdue | scheduler |
+| overdue | check_out | completed | party/operator |
+| confirmed, held | reassign | (unchanged; key := new instance) | operator |
+
+The time axis. Deadlines are policy constants (=HOLD_TTL= = 15 min,
+=CHECKIN_WINDOW= = 10 min). Each live state's deadline is discharged by an event
+that a *scheduler writes into the log*. =overdue= has no deadline; it ends at
+=check_out=.
+
+| live state | deadline | scheduler event | edge |
+|-+-+-+-|
+| held | hold.ts + 15 min | expire | held -> expired |
+| confirmed | start + 10 min | no_show | confirmed -> no-show |
+| active | end + 0 min | overdue | active -> overdue |
+
+The named sets:
+
+| set | members |
+|-+-|
+| R states | acquired, available, in-service, maintenance, out-of-order, retired |
+| V states | requested, held, confirmed, active, overdue, completed, cancelled, expired, no-show |
+| LIVE | active, confirmed, held, overdue |
+| AVAILABLE | available, in-service |
+| resource-only events | commission, fault, maintain, restore, retire |
+| reservation-only events | cancel, confirm, expire, hold, no_show, overdue, reassign, request |
+| shared events | check_in, check_out |
+| scheduler events | expire, no_show, overdue |
+
+- *Live*: =requested= is not live, because nothing has been admitted yet. The
+  four terminals are not live. =in-service= is in AVAILABLE because a resource
+  in use now can still carry live reservations for later intervals.
+- *Coupling predicate C*: for every reservation v bound to a resource instance
+  r, =v.state in LIVE => r.state in AVAILABLE=. A class key (late binding) is
+  not an instance, so C and I7 apply to v once it binds.
+- *Interval invariant I7 (non-overlap)*: for every resource instance r and every
+  pair =v != w= of reservations bound to r with =v.state, w.state in LIVE=,
+  =v.end <= w.start= or =w.end <= v.start=. The intervals are half-open. This is
+  interval scheduling: it is not a chain and not a DAG.
+- *Fencing F1*: per resource r, =fence(r)= is the highest token r has accepted
+  (initially 0). =check_in= must carry =token > fence(r)= and then sets
+  =fence(r) := token=. =check_out= must carry =token = fence(r)=. Any other token
+  is *fenced*: the event is rejected by the resource, reported, and not applied.
+  This is the distributed-lock-lease answer to stale holders: the reservation
+  carries a monotone version per resource.
+- *Late binding*: a reservation may be booked against a class key and bound to
+  an instance at =check_in=. =reassign= rebinds it as compensation. The
+  resource key on the reservation changes over its life, so the view keeps
+  =booked= (the key at =request=) and =key= (the current key) per reservation.
+
+- *(a) valid projection*: Per resource instance: =current_state= (R), =fence=,
+  and the *set of live intervals* bound to it, each with its reservation id and
+  its =current_state= (V). Per reservation: =current_state=, =booked=, =key=.
+  Plus the anomaly list.
+- *(b) wide flags SOUND?*: *no - and adding flags cannot repair it*. R is a cycle
+  (unsound, sec 2.2). V is a DAG (sound but not complete, sec 2.4). I7 is worse:
+  it is not a property of any single entity's history. It is a /binary
+  relation between reservations that share a key over the time axis/. The
+  flag / down-set projection is per-entity and unary, so no choice of flags on
+  R, on V, or on their product can express non-overlap. Neither can a
+  current_state column alone.
+- *(c) right projection*: per resource, the set of live intervals, both
+  current_state columns (R's for the resource, V's for each live interval),
+  and the coupling check C. Admission (=hold=, =reassign=, a late-binding
+  =check_in=) evaluates C and I7 against that set. The fold is still a pure
+  =[Event] -> View= catamorphism and *never* =(log, clock) -> view=. Expiry,
+  no-show and overdue are events a scheduler writes into the log. The fold's
+  only "now" is the log's own *watermark* (the maximum event-time in the log).
+  The watermark is used only to report a deadline that passed with no
+  scheduler event (X1), never to synthesize one.
+- *(d) invariants*:
+  - schema gate: =unknown_event=, =null_ts=, =dup= on the event key;
+  - I6 per resource key: arrival order vs event-time;
+  - S1 writer authority: scheduler events are scheduler-written;
+  - T2 on each machine: every step is an edge of R or V, and a shared event
+    must be legal on both;
+  - F1 fencing;
+  - B1: a late bind lands on an instance of the booked class;
+  - C1, coupling at admission: a hold, reassign or late bind onto an instance
+    outside AVAILABLE, which the write side should have rejected;
+  - I7 non-overlap at admission;
+  - X1 expiry-as-event at the watermark: a live reservation whose deadline is
+    at or before the watermark with no scheduler event;
+  - C2 compensation at the watermark: a live reservation bound to an instance
+    outside AVAILABLE with no compensating event. The resource's own clock
+    (=fault=, =maintain=) took the instance away. Compensation is an operator
+    =reassign=, an operator =cancel=, or the instance returning to AVAILABLE.
+- *What this class adds*:
+  1. *Expiry without an event*: absence by a deadline is itself an event,
+     written by a scheduler.
+  2. *Late binding to a class*: the key changes over a life, so identity is
+     the reservation id, not =(item_id, state)=, and a second entity is needed.
+  3. *Two clocks*: the resource's timeline can revoke it mid-reservation, so a
+     compensation path is required.
+  4. *Contention*: admission is transactional. It is the one place where the
+     read model cannot lag the log, so DDIA's timeliness is a hard requirement
+     rather than a nice property, and the fencing token guards against stale
+     holders.
+- *Back to sec 2.1 and the picker*: the picker's =allocated= state was already a
+  reservation, and the packet never said so. Inventory allocation (a quantity
+  of a SKU at a location: allocated (soft), reserved (hard), picked, shipped,
+  released back) is the resource-side view of that chain. The picker chain was
+  one reservation's lifecycle seen from the item. Its flag projection was sound
+  because it looked at one entity. Once two reservations compete for the same
+  on-hand quantity, the invariant becomes cross-entity (I7, with a capacity
+  sum in place of pairwise disjointness), and flags no longer suffice.
+
